==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/ˈbɪkə/}}
*: {{rhymes|ɪkə(r)}}

===Etymology 1===
{{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|bikeren|lang=enm}} ‘to attack’, from {{etyl|dum|en}} {{term|bicken|lang=nl}} ‘to stab, attack’ (modern {{term|bikken|lang=nl}} ‘to hack’), from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{recons|bikjaną|lang=gem-pro}} (compare Old English {{term|becca|lang=ang}} ‘pickax’, German {{term|picken|lang=de}} ‘to peck, pick at’, Old Norse {{term|bikkja|lang=non}} ‘to plunge into water’), from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{recons|bʰeg-|lang=ine-pro}} ‘to smash, break’.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# To [[quarrel]] in a [[tiresome]], insulting manner.
#: ''They '''bickered''' about dinner every evening.''
# To move [[tremulously]], quiver, shimmer (of a water stream, of a flame)
#*XIX cent, ''[[s:The Brook (Tennyson)|The Brook]]'', by [[w:Alfred, Lord Tennyson|Tennyson]]
#*: I come from haunts of coot and hern, / I make a sudden sally, / And sparkle out among the fern, / To '''bicker''' down a valley.

